The `StringUtils.containsNone` method from the Apache Commons Lang library is responsible for checking whether a CharSequence contains none of the specified characters in a given array of characters. Below is the snippet of the `containsNone` method:

```java
public static boolean containsNone(CharSequence cs, char[] searchChars) {
    if (cs == null || searchChars == null) {
        return true;
    }
    int csLen = cs.length();
    int searchLen = searchChars.length;
    for (int i = 0; i < csLen; i++) {
        char ch = cs.charAt(i);
        for (int j = 0; j < searchLen; j++) {
            if (searchChars[j] == ch) {
                // missing low surrogate, fine, like String.indexOf(String)
                // ch is in the Basic Multilingual Plane
                return false;
            }
        }
    }
    return true;
}
```

Based on the failing test case and the bug report, it seems that the `StringUtils.containsNone` method does not handle Unicode 2.0+ supplementary characters correctly. The report mentions that the method incorrectly matches Unicode supplementary characters.

